(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-1-specific-immunity-quiz"]={"title":"The Acquisition of Specific Immunity — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What are the FOUR things antibodies do once bound to antigen?","choices":["Phagocytosis, degranulation, chemotaxis and lysis","Fever, inflammation, swelling and pain","Recognition, processing, presentation and activation","Agglutination, opsonization, fixation, neutralization"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the four functions the binding sites perform once antigen is engaged. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What do ALL T cells produce?","choices":["Histamine","Cytokines","Antibody","Complement proteins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the helper cells especially, sending chemical messages to T cells, B cells and phagocytes. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Roughly how many genes do lymphocytes use to generate their variety of specific receptors?","choices":["About 50","About 5,000","Exactly one per lymphocyte","More than 500"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — from which millions to billions of distinct receptor-bearing cells arise. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which mnemonic gives the leukocytes from HIGHEST to lowest percentage?","choices":["Never let monkeys eat bananas","Leukocytes make everyone nervous, briefly","Basophils eat many new lymphocytes","Monocytes never eat basophil lymphocytes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, basophils. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which ending indicates a CHIMERIC monoclonal antibody?","choices":["-zumab","-omab","-umab","-ximab"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin is the MOST prevalent, and the only one crossing the placenta?","choices":["IgM","IgE","IgG","IgA"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is responsible for long-term immunity. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What does the T SUPPRESSOR cell do?","choices":["Coordinates the whole response","Mediates delayed allergy","Limits other T and B cells","Destroys virally infected cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What directs B-cell maturation?","choices":["Bone marrow stromal cells","The thymus gland","The spleen","Gut-associated lymphoid tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the stromal cells nurture the lymphocyte stem cells. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which fragment of an antibody binds the antigen?","choices":["Fc, the crystallizable fragment","The light chain alone","The constant region","Fab, the antigen-binding fragment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — there are two identical ones. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"On which chromosome are the MHC genes located?","choices":["Chromosome 21","Chromosome 6","Chromosome 1","Chromosome 11"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, clustered in a multi-gene complex of classes I, II and III. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which interleukin does the APC secrete, and what else does it do?","choices":["Interleukin 2 — it is an endogenous pyrogen","Interferon gamma — it activates macrophages","Interleukin 6 — it drives the acute-phase response","Interleukin 1"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It activates the T helper cell. It is an endogenous pyrogen that stimulates fever and inflammation. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which T cell destroys foreign or abnormal cells by secreting lytic enzymes?","choices":["The cytotoxic T cell, CD8","The T helper cell, CD4","The delayed hypersensitivity cell, TD","The T suppressor cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it especially targets virally infected cells, cancer cells, and cells from other humans and animals. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What is the secondary response also called?","choices":["The anamnestic response","The primary response","The innate response","The passive response"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Dysregulation of which interleukin is linked to lupus and rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":["Interleukin 6","Interleukin 12","Interleukin 2","Interleukin 1"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A patient is given pooled gamma globulin after a hepatitis A exposure. Which immunity is this?","choices":["Artificial passive","Artificial active","Natural passive","Natural active"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — immunotherapy with donated antibody, also used for rabies and tetanus. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How many polypeptide chains make up an immunoglobulin, and of what kinds?","choices":["One continuous chain","Four — two heavy, two light","Two — one heavy and one light","Six — three heavy and three light"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, generally Y-shaped, with variable and constant regions. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How long does PASSIVE immunization typically protect for?","choices":["About five years","Two to three months","Lifelong","About two weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — short-term protection from donated antibody. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Allergy is also known as which type of hypersensitivity?","choices":["Type IV","Type I","Type II","Type III"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which two immunoglobulins bind to B cells?","choices":["IgG and IgA","IgE and IgG","IgA and IgE","IgM and IgD"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — IgD's main function, and one IgM shares. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which human cells DO NOT carry MHC receptors?","choices":["Neutrophils","Red blood cells","Macrophages","B lymphocytes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — they are found on all cells except erythrocytes. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which of these is a recognized vaccine composition?","choices":["Purified complement proteins","Purified toxoids","Whole live virulent organisms","Pooled donor antibody"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside killed whole cells, live attenuated organisms, acellular or subunit antigens, and genetically engineered antigens. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What is distinctive about how MEMORY cells are formed?","choices":["They never divide at all","They fuse with plasma cells","They PAUSE partway through mitosis","They complete mitosis and then rest"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is why the secondary response is so much faster. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What does an ACTIVATED T helper cell produce, and what is its standing in the immune system?","choices":["Interleukin 1 — and it is the boss","Antibody — and it is the boss","Perforins — and it is the boss","Interleukin 2"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It assists the other immune cells and coordinates the response. And it is described as the boss of the immune system. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"In a healthy individual with robust immune responses, what happens to antibody titre over time?","choices":["It is undetectable after the primary response","It typically does NOT fall to zero","It returns to zero between exposures","It rises indefinitely"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin is the FIRST RESPONDER of the primary response, and an important complement fixer?","choices":["IgM","IgG","IgA","IgD"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it can bind to B cells. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What size of molecule is MOST antigenic?","choices":["Anything over 100,000 molecular weight","Small molecules under 1,000 molecular weight","Molecules of any size equally","Only nucleic acids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and most of them are large proteins. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which immunity does VACCINATION confer?","choices":["Natural active","Natural passive","Artificial active","Artificial passive"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Degree and duration of protection vary with each disease. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which receptor is found on T HELPER cells, and which on T CYTOTOXIC cells?","choices":["CD4 on helper, CD8 on cytotoxic","CD8 on helper, CD4 on cytotoxic","CD4 on both","CD8 on both"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin lines the mucosa and appears in saliva, tears, colostrum and mucus?","choices":["IgM","IgE","IgA","IgG"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — second most prevalent, conferring local immunity against enteric, respiratory and genitourinary pathogens. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What does the ending -mab signify in a drug name?","choices":["A mouse-derived antibody","A chimeric antibody","A monoclonal antibody","A humanized antibody specifically"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and these are proteins. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 58"}]};
